<handout>
    <title>(More) About Review Assessment 1</title>

    <page>

        <paragraphs >
            <title>How many things are we allowed to correct?</title>
            <p workspace=".5in">As many as you can in a 15 minute meeeting with me!
            </p>
        </paragraphs>

        <paragraphs >
            <title>Example short answer questions? How are points distributed? More focus on some chapters? </title>

            <p workspace=".5in">
                Review Assessment 1 from Winter 2026 is a good example of types of questions, points, and material distribution.
                In general, the material from Chapters 2 and 3 is more heavily focused on than Chapter 1. The review assessment
                will look roughly like checkpoints, but you may be asked to combine ideas, and there will be a proof. The
                questions with the ovals (like the one we did in Section 1.3 with triangles) will NOT be on the
                review assessment. For a practice proof, the theorem on the review assessment from Winter 2026 
                is a good example of what to expect and is repeated below. 
                A solution is provided at the end of this document (don't look at the solution before you try it!)
            </p>
        </paragraphs>
    <paragraphs>
        <title>Example Proof? </title>
        
        
            <exercise workspace=".5in">
                <statement>
                    <p>
                       Prove that for any integer <m>n</m>, if <m>n\not\equiv 0 \pmod{3}</m> then <m>n^2 \equiv 1 \pmod{3}</m>.
                    </p>
                </statement>
            </exercise>
            
        </paragraphs>

        <paragraphs >
            <title>Cheat Sheet?</title>
            <p> workspace=".5in"
                You get one 8.5"x11" sheet of paper, front and back, to use as a cheat sheet. It can be typed
                if you want. The purpose of making a cheat sheet is mostly to study, so please don't use AI to
                generate it!
            </p>
        </paragraphs>
    </page>
    <page>

        <paragraphs>
            <title>List of Things to Know About</title>

            <p>
                <ul>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            Tilings/tiling proofs
                        </p>
                    </li>

                    <li>Pigeonhole principle (simple and general form)</li>
                    <li>What to assume and prove in a direct proof</li>
                    <li>What cases you might use in a proof by cases</li>
                    <li>Find and explain counterexamples</li>
                    <li>Closure</li>
                    <li>Definition of even integer and odd integer</li>
                    <li>Definition of divides</li>
                    <li>Definition of congruence</li>
                    <li>Division algorithm</li>
                    <li>Properties of congruence</li>
                    <li>
                        Definition of subset, proper subset, equal sets and all
                        the subset symbols
                    </li>

                    <li>
                        How to prove a set is a subset of another, how to prove
                        proper subset, how to prove two sets are equal
                    </li>

                    <li>Power sets</li>
                    <li>Cartesian products</li>
                    <li>
                        Roster notation and set builder notation and translating
                        between
                    </li>

                    <li>Intersection, union, complement, set subtraction</li>
                    <li>Disjoint sets, how to prove 2 sets are disjoint</li>
                </ul>
            </p>
        </paragraphs>
    </page>

    <page>
        <paragraphs>
            <title>How do you Know Which Type of Proof?</title>

            <p>
                So far our main proof techniques are direct proofs, and proof by
                cases.
                We have also done proofs about different topics, like pigeonhole
                proofs, chessboard problems, even/odd integers, divisibility,
                congruence, and sets.
                The proof on Review Assessment 1 will not be on pigeonhole
                principle nor chessboard problems (though these topics will be
                on the Review Asssement).
                Here's some recommendations:
            </p>

            <p>
                <ol>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            My biggest recommendation: any statement you are
                            asked to prove, write what you know at the top of
                            your page and what you want to prove at the bottom
                            of the page.
                            It's hard to prove something if you don't know where
                            you are going! It's also hard to prove something 
                            if you aren't organized!
                            Then do the <q>no thinking</q> part of the proof,
                            where you write down defintions, both going forward
                            and taking backwards steps.
                        </p>
                    </li>

                    <li>
                        <p>
                            Two ways to recognize cases: (a) the statement's
                            hypothesis is something vague like
                            <q>for all integers</q> and it's not clear where to
                            start, (b) the statement has a an <q>or</q> in the
                            hypothesis.
                            Note that an <q>or</q> in the conclusion does not
                            necessarily mean you need to do a proof by cases. 
                            However, the conclusion hints at which cases
                            might be useful to consider. E.g., if the conclusion is 
                            <m>n^2 \equiv 1 \pmod{3}</m> or <m> 3 \mid n^3-n</m>, 
                            then you might want to consider cases 
                            based on the possible remainders when dividing by 3.
                        </p>
                    </li>

                    <li>
                        <p>
                            It might help to write down the general structure of
                            each type of proof.
                            Like if you were doing an even/odd proof, what was
                            the overall idea?
                            What about divisibility?
                            Congruence?
                            Each type of set proof?
                            (Like equality, subset, proper subset,
                            disjoint,etc.)
                        </p>
                    </li>

                    <li>
                        <p>
                            If you are concerned you are going in circles, grab a piece of scratch paper (will be provided).
                            Take what you have and try to write down only the things you know for sure in a <q>linear order</q>.
                            Write down what you know for sure at the top, and write what you want to prove (and it's equivalents
                            using definitions) at the bottom. 
                        </p>
                    </li>
                </ol>
            </p>
        </paragraphs>

        <paragraphs>
             <title>Sets, Curly Braces, Power Sets, Cartesian Products (LT 7)</title>

            <p>
                Each of problems 3.3-3.8 in your textbook on pages 126-127 are
                good practice problems. 
            </p>
            <p>
                See next page for more practice!
            </p>
            <p>
                Think of <m>subseteq</m> (subset) vs. <m>proper subset</m> (proper subset)as <m>\leq</m> and <m>&lt;</m>
                When you have <m>\leq</m>, the number on the left be can less than <em>or equal to</em> the number on the
                right. When you have <m>&lt;</m>, the number on the left must be strictly less than (not equal to) 
                the number on the right. THe line underneath <m>subseteq</m> means the same thing as the line under 
                <m>\leq</m>. Every proper subset is a subset. Any subset that is equal to the set itself is a subset,
                but not a proper subset.For example, <m>\{1,2\} \subset \{1,2,3\}</m> and 
                <m>\{1,2\} \subseteq \{1,2,3,\}</m>, but <m>\{1,2,3\} \subseteq \{1,2,3\}</m> and  <m>\{1,2,3\} = \{1,2,3\}</m>.
            </p>
            <p>
                See next page for more practice!
            </p>
        </paragraphs>

        <paragraphs>
           <title></title>
           
           
            <p>
                <ol>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            Consider <m> A = \{1,2\} </m>, <m>B = \{3,4\} </m>,
                            and <m>C = \{5,\{6\},7\} </m>.
                            <ol>
                                <li>
                                    <p>
                                        List all the elements of each of these
                                        sets.
                                    </p>
                                </li>
                                <li>
                                    <p>
                                        What are the sizes (or cardinality) of
                                        each of these sets?
                                        (That is, how many elements are in each
                                        set?)
                                    </p>
                                </li>

                                <li>
                                    <p>
                                        What are the cardinalities of
                                        <m>A \times B</m> and <m>B \times C</m>?
                                        What about <m>\mathcal{P}(A)</m>?
                                        <m>\mathcal{P}(B)</m>?
                                        <m>\mathcal{P}(C)</m>?
                                    </p>
                                </li>

                                <li>
                                    <p>
                                        Which of the following are true?
                                        <ol>
                                            <li>
                                                <p>
                                                    <m>5\in C</m>
                                                </p>
                                            </li>

                                            <li>
                                                <p>
                                                    <m>5 \subseteq C</m>
                                                </p>
                                            </li>

                                            <li>
                                                <p>
                                                    <m>\{5\}\in C</m>
                                                </p>
                                            </li>

                                            <li>
                                                <p>
                                                    <m>\{5\} \subseteq C</m>
                                                </p>
                                            </li>

                                            <li>
                                                <p>
                                                    <m>\{5\} \subset C</m>
                                                </p>
                                            </li>
                                        </ol>
                                    </p>
                                </li>

                                <li>
                                    <p>
                                        Which of the following are true?
                                        <ol>
                                            <li>
                                                <p>
                                                    <m>6\in C</m>,
                                                </p>
                                            </li>

                                            <li>
                                                <p>
                                                    <m>6 \subseteq C</m>
                                                </p>
                                            </li>

                                            <li>
                                                <p>
                                                    <m>\{6\}\in C</m>,
                                                </p>
                                            </li>

                                            <li>
                                                <p>
                                                    <m>\{6\} \subseteq C</m>,
                                                </p>
                                            </li>

                                            <li>
                                                <p>
                                                    <m>\{6\} \subset C</m>
                                                </p>
                                            </li>
                                        </ol>
                                    </p>
                                </li>

                                <li>
                                    <p>
                                        Which of the following are true?
                                    </p>

                                    <p>
                                        <ol>
                                            <li>
                                                <p>
                                                    <m>\{5\} \in \mathcal{P}(C)</m>
                                                </p>
                                            </li>

                                            <li>
                                                <p>
                                                    <m>\{6\} \in \mathcal{P}(C)</m>
                                                </p>
                                            </li>

                                            <li>
                                                <p>
                                                    <m>\{\{6\}\} \in \mathcal{P}(C)</m>
                                                </p>
                                            </li>

                                            <li>
                                                <p>
                                                    <m>\{5,6\} \in \mathcal{P}(C)</m>
                                                </p>
                                            </li>
                                        </ol>
                                    </p>
                                </li>

                                <li>
                                    <p>
                                        Which of the following are true?
                                        <ol>
                                            <li>
                                                <p>
                                                    Is
                                                    <m>(1,2) \in A \times B</m>?
                                                    Is
                                                    <m>\{1,2\} \in A\times B</m>?
                                                </p>
                                            </li>

                                            <li>
                                                <p>
                                                    Is
                                                    <m>(2,3) \in A\times B</m>?
                                                    Is
                                                    <m>\{2,3\} \in A\times B</m>?
                                                </p>
                                            </li>

                                            <li>
                                                <p>
                                                    Is
                                                    <m>(4,6)\in B\times C</m>?
                                                </p>
                                            </li>
                                                    <li>
                                                    <p>
                                                    Is
                                                    <m>(4,\{6\})\in B\times C</m>?
                                                </p>
                                            </li>

                                            <li>
                                                <p>
                                                    Is
                                                    <m>\{4,\{6\}\}\in B\times C</m>?
                                                </p>
                                            </li>
                                        </ol>
                                    </p>
                                </li>

                                <li>
                                    <p>
                                        <ol>
                                            <li>
                                                <p>
                                                    Is
                                                    <m>(1,2) \in \mathcal{P}(A)</m>?
                                                </p>
                                            </li>

                                            <li>
                                                <p>
                                                    Is
                                                    <m>\{1,2\} \in \mathcal{P}(A)</m>?
                                                </p>
                                            </li>
                                        </ol>
                                    </p>
                                </li>

                                <li>
                                    <ol>
                                        <li>
                                            <p>
                                                Is
                                                <m>\emptyset \in \mathcal{P}(A)</m>?
                                            </p>
                                        </li>

                                        <li>
                                            <p>
                                                Is
                                                <m>\{\} \in \mathcal{P}(A)</m>?
                                            </p>
                                        </li>

                                        <li>
                                            <p>
                                                Is
                                                <m>\{\emptyset\}\in\mathcal{P}(A)</m>?
                                            </p>
                                        </li>
                                    </ol>
                                </li>
                            </ol>
                        </p>
                    </li>
                </ol>
            </p>
        </paragraphs>
    </page>

    <page>
        <paragraphs>
            <title>Review from Earlier Chapters</title>

            <p>
                <ol>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            Explain why a chessboard with 1 square removed
                            cannot be tiled with dominoes.
                        </p>
                    </li>

                    <li>
                        <p>
                            Explain why a chessboard with 2 corner squares
                            removed cannot be tiled with dominoes.
                        </p>
                    </li>

                    <li>
                        <p>
                            Explain why the 5 standard tetris pieces not be able
                            to perfectly cover a <m>4\times 5</m> chessboard.
                            (Look up the Tetris pieces for now, I would put a
                            picture on the review assessment if this was on
                            there!)
                        </p>
                    </li>

                    <li>
                        <p>
                            How many people would we need in class to guarantee
                            2 of them were born in the same month?
                            What if we wanted 3 of them to be born in the same
                            month?
                            What if we wanted 4 of them to be born in the same
                            month?
                        </p>
                    </li>

                    <li>
                        <p>
                            The U.S.
                            population is currently abouy 350 million people.
                            Are there enough people to guarantee that 1 million
                            people have the same birthday?
                            Why or why not?
                            If not, how many people would we need to guarantee
                            that 1 million people have the same birthday?
                        </p>
                    </li>

                    <li>
                        <p>
                            How many cards would you need in your hand to
                            guarantee you had 2 cards of the same suit?
                            3 cards of the same suit?
                            4 cards of the same suit?
                        </p>
                    </li>

                    <li>
                        <p>
                            Prove that if <m>m</m> and <m>n</m> are even
                            integers, then <m>4\mid 3mn</m>.
                        </p>
                    </li>

                    <li>
                        <p>
                            Prove that, for all integers <m>a,b,x,y</m> and
                            <m>n</m> if <m>x\mid n</m> and <m>y \mid n</m> then
                            <m>n\mid xa + yb</m>.
                        </p>
                    </li>

                    <li>
                        <p>
                            Find the (unique) quotient and remainder when
                            <m>a = 15</m> and <m>m=4</m>?
                            What congurence can you state as a result of this?
                        </p>
                    </li>

                    <li>
                        <p>
                            List all the integers that are congruent to
                            <m>15 \pmod{4}</m>.
                        </p>
                    </li>

                    <li>
                        <p>
                            What is the (unique) quotient and remainder when
                            <m>a = -1</m> and <m>m=15</m>?
                        </p>
                    </li>

                    <li>
                        <p>
                            List all the integers that are congruent to
                            <m>-1 \pmod{15}</m>.
                        </p>
                    </li>

                    <li>
                        <p>
                            Someone says that when you divide <m>-18</m> by
                            <m>4</m> the reamainder is <m>-2</m>?
                            What is this person thinking?
                            What is the correct remainder?
                        </p>
                    </li>
                </ol>
            </p>
        </paragraphs>
    </page>

    <page>
       <p> Example proof (from review assessment 1 from Winter 2026). You will need to 
        turn the <q>prove that</q> statement into a theorem. You should make sure 
        to include all the important mathematical parts. For example, it's okay to not use 
        complete sentences, but it's not okay to leave out <q><m>k</m> is an integer</q> in the
        definition of even or divides.
       </p>

       <theorem>
        <statement>
            <p>
                For all integers <m>n</m> if <m>n\not\equiv 0 \pmod{3}</m> then <m>n^2 \equiv 1\pmod{3}</m>.
            </p>
        </statement>
       </theorem>
       <proof>
        <p>
            Let <m>n\in \mathbb{Z}</m> and assume <m>not\equiv 0 \pmod{3}</m>. By the division algorithm, this means
            that <m>n\equiv 1 \pmod{3}</m> or <m>n\equiv 2\pmod{3}</m>, so we consider these two cases.
        </p>

        <p>
            <term>Case 1:</term> Suppose that <m>n\equiv 1 \pmod{3}</m>. By the definition of congruence, <m>3 \mid n-1</m>
            and by the definition of divides, there exists an integer <m>k</m> such that <m>3k = n-1</m>. So <m>n = 3k+1</m>.
            By substitution and algebra,
                <md>
                   <mrow>  n^2 \amp =(3k+1)^2 </mrow>
                    <mrow>\amp =9k^2 + 6k + 1</mrow>
                    <mrow>\amp = 3(3k^2 + 2k) +1. </mrow>
                </md>
            Thus, <m>n^2-1 = 3(3k^2+2k)</m>. Since the integers are closed under addition and multiplication, <m>3k^2 + 2k\in\mathbb{Z}</m>
            and so by the definition of divides <m>3\mid n^2 -1 </m>. By the definition of congruence, <m>n^2 \equiv 1 \pmod{3}</m>. Therefore
            the theorem is true when <m>n\equiv 1 \pmod{3}</m>.
        </p>

         <p>
            <term>Case 2:</term> Suppose that <m>n\equiv 2 \pmod{3}</m>. By the definition of congruence, <m>3 \mid n-2</m>
            and by the definition of divides, there exists an integer <m>k</m> such that <m>3k = n-2</m>. So <m>n = 3k+2</m>.
            By substitution and algebra,
                <md>
                   <mrow>  n^2 \amp = (3k+2)^2</mrow>
                    <mrow> \amp = 9k^2 + 12k + 4</mrow>
                    <mrow> \amp = 9k^2+12k+3 + 1</mrow>
                    <mrow>\amp = 3(3k^2 + 4k+1) +1. </mrow>
                </md>
            Thus, <m>n^2-1 = 3(3k^2+4k+1)</m>. Since the integers are closed under addition and multiplication, <m>3k^2 + 4k+1\in\mathbb{Z}</m>
            and so by the definition of divides <m>3\mid n^2 -1 </m>. By the definition of congruence, <m>n^2 \equiv 1 \pmod{3}</m>. Therefore
            the theorem is true when <m>n\equiv 2 \pmod{3}</m>.
        </p>
        <p>
            Since the theorem is true for both <m>n\equiv 1\pmod{3}</m> and <m>n\equiv 2\pmod{3}</m>, the theorem
            is true in all cases.
        </p>
       </proof>
       
    </page>
</handout>